# Code owners for n-dx.
#
# The `main` ruleset already sets `require_code_owner_review: true`. Until this
# file existed that rule matched no path and so enforced nothing; every path
# listed below now needs an approval from @en-dash-consulting/n-devs, which is
# narrower than the repository's collaborator list.
#
# This is deliberately not a catch-all. A `*` owner would make every PR in the
# repository wait on the same team and train everyone to treat the requirement
# as noise. Listed here instead are the files where a change decides what the
# agent may execute, what reaches a credential, or what runs in CI — the places
# where a review needs someone who knows the threat model rather than whoever
# is free.
#
# Last match wins, so order matters: later entries override earlier ones.

# ── What runs in CI, and with what identity ─────────────────────────────────
# Workflow files can mint tokens, read secrets, and publish to npm. A change
# here is a change to the trust boundary, not to the build.
/.github/                       @en-dash-consulting/n-devs
/.github/workflows/             @en-dash-consulting/n-devs

# ── Supply chain ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `.npmrc` carries ignore-scripts and minimum-release-age; relaxing either
# re-opens install-time execution and freshly-published-package attacks. The
# lockfile is where a dependency-confusion swap would land.
/.npmrc                         @en-dash-consulting/n-devs
/pnpm-lock.yaml                 @en-dash-consulting/n-devs
/package.json                   @en-dash-consulting/n-devs

# ── What the agent may execute and read ─────────────────────────────────────
# The guard decides which commands run and which paths are writable;
# repo-trust decides whether a checkout's own config is honoured; child-env
# decides which environment variables reach a child process.
/packages/hench/src/guard/                      @en-dash-consulting/n-devs
/packages/llm-client/src/repo-trust.ts          @en-dash-consulting/n-devs
/packages/llm-client/src/child-env.ts           @en-dash-consulting/n-devs

# ── Policy gates ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These are the tests that fail a PR for crossing an architectural or
# supply-chain boundary. Weakening one is how a later change crosses it quietly.
/tests/e2e/architecture-policy.test.js          @en-dash-consulting/n-devs
/tests/e2e/domain-isolation.test.js             @en-dash-consulting/n-devs
/scripts/check-obfuscated-code.mjs              @en-dash-consulting/n-devs
/scripts/check-lockfile-integrity.mjs           @en-dash-consulting/n-devs

# ── Security policy ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/SECURITY.md                    @en-dash-consulting/n-devs
/.github/CODEOWNERS             @en-dash-consulting/n-devs
